          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Social and Economic Policies</strong><br>\nTo stabilize his rule, Odoacer implemented various policies aimed at restoring economic stability and social order. He redistributed land and provided support to Roman citizens affected by the changes of the time. His policies were a blend of Roman law and Germanic customs.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Odoacer's Downfall</strong><br>\nThe end of Odoacer's rule came when the Eastern Roman Emperor Zeno arranged for Theodoric the Great to invade Italy. In 493 A.D., after a three-year siege of Ravenna, Odoacer surrendered to Theodoric, who had promised to share power, but instead killed Odoacer and became the sole ruler of Italy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nOdoacer is often remembered as the first barbarian king of Italy, marking the transition from Roman to Germanic control over the peninsula. His rule signified the changing landscape of power in Europe and the beginning of the Middle Ages. His influence continued to be felt in the blending of Germanic and Roman cultures.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
